diagnosis_text:
The Clos fabric is healthy and inter-rack L3 reachability is working. End-to-end tests show pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) can reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT in both directions. The alleged “rack’s top switch lost both of its links upward” would correspond to leaf_router_0_1’s uplinks to the spines; however, both uplinks are operational: leaf_router_0_1 eth0 (172.16.0.7/31 to spine_router_0_0) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31 to spine_router_0_1) report Link detected: yes. BGP adjacencies are established on all relevant nodes: leaf_router_0_1 is peered with both spines (State/PfxRcd=1 for each), spines have routes to 10.0.1.0/24 via the correct leaf next-hops, and leaf_router_0_0 has a BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via spine 0_0. PCs have correct defaults to their leaves. This explains why your pings now succeed—both forward and return paths are present and advertised.

Localization: No failed interfaces or sessions found on leaf_router_0_1, spine_router_0_0, or spine_router_0_1. Up/Down timers (~2 minutes) suggest recent establishment, consistent with a transient during fabric/L3 bring-up.

Root cause: No current fault; any earlier outage was transient and has self-cleared—there is no evidence of a dual-uplink failure on leaf_router_0_1 at this time.

Corrective fix: No action required for restoration. If the issue recurs intermittently, enable/verify BFD on leaf–spine sessions, collect FRR and interface logs on event, and add monitoring alerts for BGP and link state to catch future flaps. === END ===